  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 1.4. Strengthened social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Since November 2020, National social dialogue committee (CNDS) strengthened its legal framework by reviewing the National Social Dialogue Charter to include new mechanisms of social dialogue at provincial level and provide with operational guidance to social dialogue mechanisms at company level. 

The ILO constituents in Burundi signed in July 2021 an MoU/declaration on how each group is going to contribute to advance social dialogue at workplace by responding to the gaps encountered during the ten year experience of implementation of the National social dialogue charter. 

Tripartite constituents created social dialogue committees at company/public service level from July 2021 (this is ongoing activity led by Association of Burundi Employers/Association des Employeurs du Burundi - AEB) to prevent and handle labour disputes including those resulting from the impact of covid-19 at work place. Through the committees, workers and employers can now access mediation framework at their respective workplaces and their disputes settled peacefully before they reach courts Trainings workshops and dialogue sessions) aimed at strengthening the capacity of the National council for social dialogue (CNDS) to serve as an effective and inclusive platform for policy consultations between Government and the social partners on national strategies, including to address the impacts of COVID-19 and accelerate social and economic recovery

The ILO further provided capacity building for new members of existing social dialogue organs have been conducted increasing creation of sectoral social dialogue committees from 9 to 16.  

The using RBSA recourses supported CNDS to conduct an evaluation of actuarial status of implementation of social dialogue provisions and their related mechanisms. This led to the agreement and signing of the MoU amongst constituents in July 20212 

The ILO supported the CNDS to conduct high profile capacity building workshops in February 2021, for members of parliament, ministers , Government&apos;s senior executives and social partners on social dialogue related ILS  as part of strengthening of social dialogue mechanisms in Burundi</narrative>
   </description>
